It also keeps our roses red and our corn stalks high.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
It falls from above, and it was the big prize in a war.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
It's the recycled waste of restaurants, restrooms, and trash bins.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
It's dredged from ancient seeds and drenched in modern solutions.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Dripped, dropped, or dumped.

7
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:41,001
dumped. Now, fertilizer on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,215
Every day around the world, six and a half billion of us need to eat.

9
00:00:49,216 --> 00:00:50,500
For food, we rely

10
00:00:50,501 --> 00:00:55,157
on the earth, where the soil provides nutrients to feed our crops.

11
00:00:55,158 --> 00:00:56,780
But those nutrients are

12
00:00:56,781 --> 00:00:57,780
limited.

13
00:00:57,781 --> 00:01:03,780
Every time we harvest corn or tomatoes or lettuce or anything from that field, we're

14
00:01:03,781 --> 00:01:08,420
also harvesting the nutrients that are in that crop. So when we continually harvest

15
00:01:08,421 --> 00:01:13,100
things from the field, we have to replace them where the soil becomes depleted.

16
00:01:13,101 --> 00:01:20,100
Enter fertilizers, the nutrient-rich elixirs that make it possible to reuse the land.

17
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:23,300
to grow the crops that feed the world.

18
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:32,280
In 2006, we spread over 150 million tons of fertilizer around the globe.

19
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:36,360
It's estimated that without it, 2 billion of us would starve.

20
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:43,740
Fertilizer's power is contained in three key nutrients,

21
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:46,180
all with a vital role to play in plant health.

22
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:52,980
nitrogen promotes growth by driving photosynthesis and transferring light into usable chemical energy

23
00:01:52,981 --> 00:01:59,060
phosphate contains compounds that both store and release life-sustaining energy within the plant

24
00:01:59,061 --> 00:02:03,980
on the cellular level and potassium helps to regulate the water content of a plant

25
00:02:03,981 --> 00:02:09,720
by controlling the opening and closing of its pores but before any of them become fertilizer

26
00:02:09,721 --> 00:02:15,500
the big three nutrients have to be harvested and potash corp is doing just that at its pcs

27
00:02:15,501 --> 00:02:22,254
phosphate mine in Aurora, North Carolina. This is where fertilizer begins.

28
00:02:22,255 --> 00:02:24,080
We bring it from the

29
00:02:24,081 --> 00:02:29,140
ancient bed where it was naturally deposited back onto the surface where we can use it in today's

30
00:02:29,141 --> 00:02:36,542
world. Millions of years ago, this 250-acre hole was part of an ocean.

31
00:02:36,543 --> 00:02:39,080
Now it's a graveyard for

32
00:02:39,081 --> 00:02:43,236
the ancient sharks, whales, and various sea creatures that died here.

33
00:02:43,237 --> 00:02:44,260
Pulverized within

34
00:02:44,261 --> 00:02:48,880
their remains is phosphate, a nutrient picked up in the diet and stored in bones.

35
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:55,280
We're now standing 30 feet below sea level, reaching down into an ancient deposit of marine

36
00:02:55,281 --> 00:02:57,155
life that laid the phosphorus down.

37
00:02:57,156 --> 00:02:59,780
We're mining that phosphate out, bringing it back

38
00:02:59,781 --> 00:03:03,587
to the surface where we can then use it.

39
00:03:03,588 --> 00:03:08,060
He's now swinging around and placing it under a

40
00:03:08,061 --> 00:03:10,460
pile here where we make it usable as a fertilizer.

41
00:03:10,461 --> 00:03:17,460
Geologists estimate that this deposit will continue to supply phosphate for at least 90 more years.

42
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:20,000
But it doesn't come easily.

43
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:26,100
It takes some of the sturdiest and most powerful machines ever made to pry it from the earth.

44
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:33,160
The boom on the length of a drag line is about the length of a football field.

45
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:39,680
300 feet long, leading down to the drag line itself, which is about a 10 million pound machine.

46
00:03:39,681 --> 00:03:43,280
8,000 horsepower runs it through an electrical cord.

47
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:46,220
These are the chain links that lead to the dragline bucket.

48
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:48,680
Each link is about the length of my arm or so.

49
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:54,800
The dragline bucket is really the primary tool that we use to dig the phosphate.

50
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:58,160
This particular one is about the size of a two-car garage,

51
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:03,620
75 cubic yards, and scoops about 60 tons of material with each scoop.

52
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:08,240
Manned and moving every hour of every day,

53
00:04:08,241 --> 00:04:13,240
This drag line pulls out over 4 million tons of phosphate ore annually.

54
00:04:13,241 --> 00:04:20,240
Once the ore is up, it gets wet for its journey into the modern world of fertilizer production.

55
00:04:20,241 --> 00:04:28,240
We then use water pressure, 250 VSI water pressure to slurry this water, to mix it with the ore,

56
00:04:28,241 --> 00:04:34,240
and then we pump it, run it through these pumps, down through this pipeline, and all the way to the mill.

57
00:04:34,241 --> 00:04:41,240
At the mill, the slurry enters a tangled maze of pipes, pumps and waterways, all designed

58
00:04:41,241 --> 00:04:45,240
to isolate phosphate from earth and turn it into usable fertilizer.

59
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:52,240
The phosphate or slurry from the mine is pumped up to the mill and brought through

60
00:04:52,241 --> 00:04:57,240
this screening process where the oversized material is removed.

61
00:04:57,241 --> 00:05:03,240
The slurry hops across a vibrating screen where the finer material falls through slits.

62
00:05:03,241 --> 00:05:05,780
The larger material continues to march toward the edge.

63
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:11,360
That finer material is what we're trying to recover.

64
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:17,100
The oversized material we do not process, and it goes off the end of the screen.

65
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:22,900
And one of the most common things that we find coming off the screen are shark's teeth.

66
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:30,820
After a shiver, the phosphate and sand are thickened with plant oil into a kind of milkshake.

67
00:05:30,821 --> 00:05:35,820
This gives the phosphate something to cling on to before it enters the paddle line.

68
00:05:35,821 --> 00:05:43,820
The phosphate and sand mixture that has been conditioned now enters these flotation cells.

69
00:05:43,821 --> 00:05:49,820
We introduce air bubbles into the bottom of the tank and we skim off the phosphate from the top

70
00:05:49,821 --> 00:05:54,820
because the phosphate has an affinity for that air bubble and attaches to it.

71
00:05:54,821 --> 00:05:58,740
You can see a lot of phosphate being recovered by this first cell.

72
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:03,900
As we move down the cell, you see less and less phosphate being recovered.

73
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:10,120
The phosphate is still not quite ready for plants to digest.

74
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,840
It needs a little boost from science.

75
00:06:13,660 --> 00:06:19,140
So it's off to another facility, where it's combined with sulfuric acid to make phosphoric acid,

76
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:23,160
the same stuff used to remove rust and add the bite in soft drinks.

77
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:31,720
Piped inside a rotating drum, the phosphoric acid joins some ammonia and a batch of already made granular fertilizer.

78
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:35,700
Once it's all mixed together, the phosphate fertilizer is complete.

79
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,060
And here we have one of our final products, a granular ammonium phosphate.

80
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:47,420
Through mining, we're able to bring that phosphate ore body to the surface.

81
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:52,000
And then through the chemical processes, we're able to make that a soluble product

82
00:06:52,001 --> 00:06:59,960
so that we have this granular fertilizer product that can be used to help feed the world.

83
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:04,660
But phosphate is just one of three key nutrients required by plants.

84
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:08,800
The second is potassium.

85
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:14,160
It too is mined from ancient deposits, these formed by evaporated seawater.

86
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:19,920
Except this nutrient is buried in dirt more than 3,000 feet underground.

87
00:07:19,921 --> 00:07:28,000
Here, boring machines work around the clock to bring it to the surface.

88
00:07:28,001 --> 00:07:33,000
Inside the mill, the raw material is crushed and cleaned to separate clay and salt from

89
00:07:33,001 --> 00:07:34,880
the potassium.

90
00:07:34,881 --> 00:07:39,060
The potassium is then dried and sized.

91
00:07:39,061 --> 00:07:42,920
Nitrogen is the third and most used of the big three nutrients.

92
00:07:42,921 --> 00:07:44,520
It's pulled from the air.

93
00:07:44,521 --> 00:07:48,560
Approximately 80% of the atmosphere that we breathe is nitrogen.

94
00:07:48,561 --> 00:07:51,060
However, that's not in a form that plants can use.

95
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:58,300
Nitrogen fertilizers are made by reacting natural gas, steam, and air to form anhydrous ammonia,

96
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:01,200
which is then reacted with air to make nitric acid.

97
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:06,200
Then nitric acid is combined with anhydrous ammonia to make ammonium nitrate,

98
00:08:06,980 --> 00:08:09,560
one of several soluble nitrogen-based fertilizers.

99
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:17,560
But just as ammonia nitrate can help build life, it can also take it away.

100
00:08:17,561 --> 00:08:19,880
as a potential agent in explosives.

101
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:25,940
Ironically, this characteristic was the catalyst for the commercial fertilizer industry.

102
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:31,700
During World War II, American munitions factories worked overtime

103
00:08:31,701 --> 00:08:34,360
to produce stockpiles of ammonium nitrate for bombs.

104
00:08:35,540 --> 00:08:38,340
After the war, the factories were stuck with a surplus.

105
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:41,880
They retooled to manufacture nitrogen-based fertilizers.

106
00:08:43,220 --> 00:08:46,340
Following World War II, as nitrogen fertilizers became available,

107
00:08:46,341 --> 00:08:51,340
farmers now had an affordable source of nitrogen that could be used to improve crop yields.

108
00:08:51,341 --> 00:08:56,340
But mining the earth and air aren't the only ways to make fertilizer.

