195
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:36,000
It's dinner time outside the Frio Batcave in Garner Park, Texas.

196
00:15:37,340 --> 00:15:39,960
Every day at dusk between April and November,

197
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:45,060
40 million Mexican free-tailed bats leave their home for the evening feast.

198
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:52,620
By the time they return, just before daybreak, they'll have consumed nearly 500 tons of mosquitoes.

199
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:02,940
Back inside the cave, the bats will hang around for a bit and wait for nature's call.

200
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:07,880
Then they'll litter the floor with some of the most powerful poop on Earth.

201
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:14,140
A fertilizer called guano, Spanish for manure.

202
00:16:15,820 --> 00:16:18,240
John Sheely is here to harvest it.

203
00:16:20,460 --> 00:16:25,680
This cave has 26 1⁄2 miles of explored passages.

204
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:32,420
We're currently going in, I would have to say, the third room,

205
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:36,540
making the turn down into the main room,

206
00:16:36,541 --> 00:16:39,740
which is roughly two football fields in size.

207
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:42,320
From talking to old timers in the past,

208
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:49,040
they have cored down in this cave and gone 200 feet,

209
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:51,520
and they were still in guano in this room.

210
00:16:53,380 --> 00:16:55,820
Harvesting guano hasn't changed for centuries.

211
00:16:56,440 --> 00:16:59,020
It's straight from the bats behind and into the can.

212
00:17:00,020 --> 00:17:00,720
Why?

213
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:05,320
Because as is guano is loaded with organic nitrogen, potassium, and phosphate.

214
00:17:05,321 --> 00:17:09,920
not to mention 13 trace elements essential for plant growth.

215
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:13,620
All manure is a product of what that animal ate.

216
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:16,860
These mammals are eating meat.

217
00:17:17,320 --> 00:17:21,980
They're eating toxic-free insects, moss and mosquitoes.

218
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:25,320
We know where they went.

219
00:17:25,660 --> 00:17:27,240
We know where they live.

220
00:17:28,160 --> 00:17:32,360
And there's nothing in here that can harm us.

221
00:17:32,361 --> 00:17:36,440
And don't let its light, fluffy texture fool you.

222
00:17:37,480 --> 00:17:39,780
Guano is a rare, soluble organic fertilizer.

223
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:45,100
When a solution of it hits the soil, its nutrients move like a bat out of hell.

224
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:51,280
But bet guano isn't the only highly-priced fertilizer found in vast deposits.

225
00:17:52,760 --> 00:17:55,300
Birds also drop a mighty guano bomb.

226
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,640
So powerful, in fact, that a war was actually fought over it.

227
00:18:02,360 --> 00:18:06,260
In the mid-1800s, the world's population was growing rapidly.

228
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:10,560
Without a chemical fertilizer industry to meet food demand,

229
00:18:11,060 --> 00:18:13,240
organic fertilizer was a precious commodity.

230
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:16,580
Bird guano was one of the most popular.

231
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:22,800
In 1840, Chile, Bolivia, and Peru hit the guano jackpot

232
00:18:22,801 --> 00:18:25,720
when they discovered that along their western coasts

233
00:18:25,721 --> 00:18:27,920
