234
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:31,800
Seabirds will go out, catch fish during the day,

235
00:18:31,801 --> 00:18:35,680
come back at night and the excrement around their nests accumulates.

236
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:41,340
The dried excrement can be 20, 30, 40 feet deep

237
00:18:41,341 --> 00:18:43,420
as it accumulates over the centuries.

238
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:46,740
And in these low rainfall environments, it never gets washed away.

239
00:18:47,680 --> 00:18:51,160
The guano gold mine was measured at several million tons,

240
00:18:51,860 --> 00:18:54,160
nearly a billion dollars worth on the open market.

241
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:58,360
It wasn't long before the battle for bird droppings was on.

242
00:18:58,361 --> 00:19:06,914
In 1879, Chile went to war with Bolivia. Bolivia allied itself with Peru.

243
00:19:06,915 --> 00:19:09,140
The war lasted five

244
00:19:09,141 --> 00:19:14,589
years, and when it was over, Chile had kicked the guano out of its two neighbors.

245
00:19:14,590 --> 00:19:15,290
The map

246
00:19:15,291 --> 00:19:20,240
was redrawn. Bolivia is now landlocked because of the war over bird poop.

247
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:24,385
The spoils would only last so long.

248
00:19:24,386 --> 00:19:28,000
By the 20th century, the guano deposit was nearly depleted.

249
00:19:28,360 --> 00:19:35,980
Fortunately for plants and farmers everywhere, bat and bird guano aren't the only stinky

250
00:19:35,981 --> 00:19:39,760
stuff around.

251
00:19:39,761 --> 00:19:43,960
Today there's a new dew rising, worm castings.

252
00:19:43,961 --> 00:19:54,140
Through a process called vermicomposting, worms are fed a steady diet of cow manure.

253
00:19:54,141 --> 00:19:57,120
The worms in turn will excrete a powerful fertilizer of their own.

254
00:19:59,300 --> 00:20:06,920
The idea is that by double dipping, organic matter is broken down to such an extent that it releases nutrients more quickly to plants.

255
00:20:08,760 --> 00:20:12,480
Warning. The process is not for the faint of heart.

256
00:20:15,480 --> 00:20:16,640
Here's how it works.

257
00:20:17,460 --> 00:20:21,740
At Coyne Farms, a highly respected enterprise in upstate New York,

258
00:20:22,740 --> 00:20:27,620
dairy cows are busy making the same mess that's been fertilizing fields since the beginning of crop production.

259
00:20:31,880 --> 00:20:35,800
Early farmers made the observation that where there was manure,

260
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:39,760
the plants around that organic matter are stronger and look better.

261
00:20:41,060 --> 00:20:43,820
Working back and forth across this slimy floor,

262
00:20:43,821 --> 00:20:50,820
A mechanical pooper scooper slowly scrapes the cow manure toward the center of the barn.

263
00:20:50,821 --> 00:20:56,160
These piles of dung may look repulsive, but to Tom Hurley, owner of Worm Power in Avon,

264
00:20:56,161 --> 00:21:02,700
New York, this barn floor is overflowing with nutrients, just waiting to be harnessed.

265
00:21:02,701 --> 00:21:04,860
This is where the vermicomposting process begins.

266
00:21:04,861 --> 00:21:06,860
This is my feedstock right here.

267
00:21:06,861 --> 00:21:09,620
Back into the cows, the cow flop falls down in the aisle.

268
00:21:09,621 --> 00:21:13,440
The manure is pushed down the aisle, down in here into the central way where it falls into the sump.

269
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:15,140
It comes down in here.

270
00:21:15,480 --> 00:21:19,320
There's 80,000 gallons of manure, and it's being agitated, pumped around and around and around.

271
00:21:20,220 --> 00:21:24,360
The pool of poop is filled with traces of nitrogen, phosphate, and potassium.

272
00:21:25,140 --> 00:21:27,860
But worms like their dinner on the dryer side.

273
00:21:28,740 --> 00:21:33,500
It's pumped up, put into this big hopper here where it's going to be fed into the separator.

274
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:39,580
The separator is nothing more than a giant squeeze press, just like when you're making wine or apple cider.

275
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:43,060
It's squeezed. We get the solids here out of the end.

276
00:21:43,061 --> 00:21:48,280
You can see it's all fibrous from the bedding from the cow and also from their diet.

277
00:21:48,281 --> 00:21:51,860
And then the juice comes out the pipe down here at the bottom.

278
00:21:53,940 --> 00:21:57,880
The separated liquid travels to a million-gallon manure lagoon.

279
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:05,920
Later, so as not to waste a drop, the nose-rotting liquid will be sent to a corn pasture

280
00:22:05,921 --> 00:22:08,700
to fertilize feed for the same cows that made it.

281
00:22:09,620 --> 00:22:14,620
Meanwhile, the separated dry manure is trucked to the vermicompost plant.

282
00:22:14,621 --> 00:22:20,620
But before it's fed to the worms, it needs to be broken down into smaller portions.

283
00:22:20,621 --> 00:22:27,620
The best way to do that is to compost it with an active organic material, like rotting cattle feed.

284
00:22:27,621 --> 00:22:31,620
Normally, the manure would take several months to decompose.

285
00:22:31,621 --> 00:22:33,620
That's where the worms come in.

286
00:22:33,621 --> 00:22:37,620
Here we are. This is the heart of the vermicomposting operation.

287
00:22:37,621 --> 00:22:41,280
In this room, we have about 7 million earthworms living and breeding.

288
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,240
Each one of these beds is about 1,000 square feet of surface area.

289
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:49,420
We've taken that manure, we've composted it, and now we're feeding it to the worms.

290
00:22:49,860 --> 00:22:51,500
The worms are multiplying in here.

291
00:22:51,580 --> 00:22:55,920
All they're doing is eating and breeding, and our job is to keep them fat, dumb, and happy.

292
00:22:55,921 --> 00:23:01,920
They're in here eating away like mad, and you can see we've got lots of earthworms.

293
00:23:03,520 --> 00:23:06,420
Food and sex in a specially made trough.

294
00:23:06,421 --> 00:23:11,440
This may seem like worm heaven, but these lucky little critters are providing a crucial

295
00:23:11,441 --> 00:23:14,840
step in breaking down the big three nutrients.

296
00:23:14,841 --> 00:23:16,400
They have a gizzard like a chicken.

297
00:23:16,401 --> 00:23:17,540
They ingest the particle.

298
00:23:17,541 --> 00:23:19,040
They fragment it up.

299
00:23:19,041 --> 00:23:21,260
They accelerate decomposition.

300
00:23:21,261 --> 00:23:25,040
Regular composting, it would take six to nine months to get a stable end product.

301
00:23:25,041 --> 00:23:27,180
21 days ago, this was manure.

302
00:23:27,181 --> 00:23:31,300
40 days in here, and then I'm all done.

303
00:23:31,301 --> 00:23:34,860
Inside the trough, the worms work in a counterflow system.

304
00:23:34,861 --> 00:23:38,100
The original compost is spread on the top of the bed.

305
00:23:38,101 --> 00:23:40,360
The worms then move up to eat.

306
00:23:40,361 --> 00:23:44,260
After digestion, they expel particles called castings.

307
00:23:44,261 --> 00:23:48,620
A winch then pulls a bar through the trough which skims off a layer of castings to the

308
00:23:48,621 --> 00:23:50,900
bottom.

309
00:23:50,901 --> 00:23:57,180
Compost is then added back on top of the trough and the process continues.

310
00:23:57,181 --> 00:24:02,580
Forty-five days later, the vermicastings are calmly shaken onto a conveyor below and the

311
00:24:02,581 --> 00:24:07,580
final product slowly rides off to the packaging plant.

312
00:24:07,581 --> 00:24:11,180
Here's the black rain falling.

313
00:24:11,181 --> 00:24:15,460
60 days ago that was manure. Now it's beautiful, rich,

314
00:24:15,461 --> 00:24:19,100
earthy smelling material. Plant the seed in it, jump back,

315
00:24:19,101 --> 00:24:21,740
before the shoot comes and hits you in the eye.

316
00:24:21,741 --> 00:24:26,940
Worms can poop great fertilizer. And cows and bats can too.

317
00:24:26,941 --> 00:24:30,300
So what about the hairless ape?

318
00:24:30,301 --> 00:24:33,717
In 1856, Congress passed the Guano Islands Act, which gave U.S.

319
00:24:33,718 --> 00:24:40,660
citizens the right to possess any island with guano deposits, as long as it was not under any foreign government's jurisdiction.

320
00:24:41,340 --> 00:24:43,500
Fertilizer will return on Modern Marvels.

321
00:24:45,260 --> 00:24:48,480
We now return to Fertilizer on Modern Marvels.

322
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:55,476
At one of New Jersey's finest golf courses, Superintendent Roger Stewart Jr.

323
00:24:55,477 --> 00:24:59,100
is making sure his fairways and greens are in peak condition.

324
00:25:00,300 --> 00:25:06,300
Because here alone, he and his crew will feed the grass with nearly 100 tons of prime organic fertilizer.

325
00:25:07,700 --> 00:25:13,920
The thing we really like about this fertilizer is that it's a natural organic product.

326
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:19,600
Because of the slow release rate, the fertilizer lasts a long time, and we don't have to go out as often to put it on.

327
00:25:21,440 --> 00:25:23,740
In New Jersey, the weather can be harsh.

328
00:25:24,680 --> 00:25:27,100
Keeping the grass green requires a robust fertilizer.

329
00:25:28,060 --> 00:25:30,260
Something like human waste.

330
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:33,700
It really doesn't bother me that it comes from human waste.

331
00:25:33,980 --> 00:25:38,380
The product that comes out here doesn't look anything like the human waste did when it started out.

332
00:25:39,780 --> 00:25:45,900
Actually, it looked like this, a river of mortal muck slithering through the New Jersey sewage system.

333
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:51,060
On any given day, the wave moves through morning rush hour and picks up steam

334
00:25:51,061 --> 00:25:56,000
until finally the sludge tsunami enters the Ocean County sewage treatment plant.

335
00:25:56,001 --> 00:26:03,400
In any wastewater treatment plant, our flow rates during the course of the day pretty much follow people's normal habits.

336
00:26:03,980 --> 00:26:08,240
During the nighttime, early morning hours, after midnight, our flows are very low.

337
00:26:08,580 --> 00:26:14,580
And then during the day, as people become active, 6, 7 o'clock in the morning, our flows pick up.

338
00:26:15,440 --> 00:26:19,900
The first order of business is to remove any non-organic waste with a climber screen.

339
00:26:19,901 --> 00:26:30,640
The mechanical bar screens screen out just about any type of material that's found that can be flushed down the toilet or go down a drain.

340
00:26:30,641 --> 00:26:44,173
And it's an array of rag material, some tissue paper, all-purpose cleaners, condoms, feminine applicators, anything that could potentially be harmful to our downstream

341
00:26:44,174 --> 00:26:46,280
treatment plant equipment.

342
00:26:46,281 --> 00:26:54,520
downstream the filtered sludge enters a dome structure called a primary clarifier inside

343
00:26:54,521 --> 00:26:59,420
is a circular tank holding six hundred and eighty six thousand gallons of human wastewater

344
00:26:59,421 --> 00:27:07,520
the stench is enough to make a grown man cry the clarifier is designed to let gravity do most of

345
00:27:07,521 --> 00:27:15,680
the work heavy particles fall to the bottom of the tank oil-based liquids rise to the top what

346
00:27:15,681 --> 00:27:18,680
What you see on the surface is a skimmer mechanism.

347
00:27:18,681 --> 00:27:25,960
Its purpose is to remove any floating grease, oil, fats, anything that passes through that

348
00:27:25,961 --> 00:27:30,620
our bar screens don't catch that floats on the surface.

349
00:27:30,621 --> 00:27:34,840
But the real action is taking place below the surface.

350
00:27:34,841 --> 00:27:39,200
The primary tank has a floor that's sloped toward a hopper in the center.

351
00:27:39,201 --> 00:27:43,680
A few inches above the bottom, an iron rake extends in both directions to the edge of

352
00:27:43,681 --> 00:27:44,680
the tank.

353
00:27:44,681 --> 00:27:51,120
At the rate of 9 feet per minute, the rake moves around the tank and slowly pushes the heavy sludge toward the center.

354
00:27:52,280 --> 00:27:55,180
From there, it's pumped into anaerobic digesters.

355
00:27:56,960 --> 00:28:00,960
The sludge will then mix in the digester tanks for 15 to 30 days.

356
00:28:02,340 --> 00:28:08,220
Microorganisms will begin to break it down and stabilize the solids, killing unwanted bacteria in the process.

357
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:11,860
Then it needs to lose some water weight.

358
00:28:11,861 --> 00:28:18,640
When the sludge comes across the belt initially, it's at 4% siloids or 96% water.

359
00:28:19,020 --> 00:28:26,820
This section is called the gravity belt section, and the gravity belt section allows the water to freely flow through the fabric.

360
00:28:27,560 --> 00:28:30,360
And it's whatever comes out by gravity all by itself.

361
00:28:30,460 --> 00:28:36,540
It will drop out here and drop straight down, and that water is collected and sent back to the head of our plant.

362
00:28:36,541 --> 00:28:42,840
After gravity has done everything it can, a high-pressure roller squeezes out another

363
00:28:42,841 --> 00:28:44,840
round of water.

364
00:28:44,841 --> 00:28:48,780
The solids are starting to look like, and feel, like fertilizer.

365
00:28:48,781 --> 00:28:53,960
At this point in time, the sludge has already been treated.

366
00:28:53,961 --> 00:28:56,000
It's virtually bacteria-free.

367
00:28:56,001 --> 00:29:01,540
Our fertilizer is 5% nitrogen, 5% phosphorus, and 0% potassium.

368
00:29:01,541 --> 00:29:06,540
Plus there are other micronutrients in here also which are good for plant growth.

369
00:29:06,541 --> 00:29:11,540
One more hard push through a press and it's out.

370
00:29:11,541 --> 00:29:14,540
In the industry the waste is now called cake.

371
00:29:14,541 --> 00:29:17,540
And this cake is headed for a blow-dry.

372
00:29:17,541 --> 00:29:23,540
We are now going to mix it with some already dried material, which is in that particular bin over there.

373
00:29:23,541 --> 00:29:26,540
The two of them come together in this mixer.

374
00:29:26,541 --> 00:29:33,500
In this mixer, we will take the cake and the solids up to 60% dry material.

375
00:29:34,420 --> 00:29:40,280
At that point in time, the pellets are preformed and ready to go into the dryer.

376
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:45,520
Three passes through the 1,200 degree Fahrenheit dryer,

377
00:29:45,920 --> 00:29:49,260
and any bacteria strong enough to make it to this point are laid to rest.

378
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:52,380
And that's a good thing,

379
00:29:52,381 --> 00:29:59,780
because untreated human waste can transport viral and bacterial diseases like hepatitis, typhoid, and cholera.

380
00:30:00,780 --> 00:30:05,840
On final approach, the cake is finally screened to order, and here it comes.

381
00:30:06,960 --> 00:30:10,520
This is the final product. It started out as the flush of a toilet.

382
00:30:11,240 --> 00:30:16,780
It is now a commodity for the fertilizer industry, made to the specifications of that industry.

383
00:30:16,781 --> 00:30:23,780
Even before modern processing, human waste was commonly used as a fertilizer.

384
00:30:23,781 --> 00:30:27,780
Only in the ancient world, deposits were more direct.

385
00:30:27,781 --> 00:30:33,780
The ancient Greeks actually did canals that would leave the city carrying the human waste out to the olive groves.

386
00:30:33,781 --> 00:30:36,780
Today, the use of sewage sludge is controversial.

387
00:30:36,781 --> 00:30:42,780
Unlike the Greeks' canals, America's sewer systems carry more than human waste.

388
00:30:42,781 --> 00:30:43,481
waste.

389
00:30:43,482 --> 00:30:48,860
The constituents in the sewer treatment plant then contain whatever is flowing into the

390
00:30:48,861 --> 00:30:49,633
sewer that day.

391
00:30:49,634 --> 00:30:53,860
It may be waste from a household, it could be from an industry, it could be runoff

392
00:30:53,861 --> 00:30:55,523
from a parking lot or a street.

393
00:30:55,524 --> 00:30:58,420
Still, the government and the industry are comfortable

394
00:30:58,421 --> 00:31:03,735
that with treatment, sewage sludge fertilizer is perfectly safe.

395
00:31:03,736 --> 00:31:06,060
In 1991, the industry wisely

396
00:31:06,061 --> 00:31:12,973
decided on a name change. Human waste and sewage sludge became biosolids.

397
00:31:12,974 --> 00:31:15,340
In 2006, three and a half

398
00:31:15,341 --> 00:31:19,759
million tons of dried biosolids were sold as fertilizer in the U.S.

399
00:31:19,760 --> 00:31:21,540
Sounds like a lot, but it's

400
00:31:21,541 --> 00:31:24,445
still less than one percent of the overall market.

401
00:31:24,446 --> 00:31:27,060
That percentage may increase if others are as

402
00:31:27,061 --> 00:31:30,630
satisfied as the superintendent of the Princeton golf course.

403
00:31:30,631 --> 00:31:32,620
There's certainly no danger to any

404
00:31:32,621 --> 00:31:34,080
of the people who play golf here.

405
00:31:34,660 --> 00:31:36,840
I think our members want us to act

406
00:31:36,841 --> 00:31:38,840
in an environmentally responsible way.

407
00:31:39,340 --> 00:31:41,940
And we think we're participating in that cause

408
00:31:41,941 --> 00:31:45,020
and doing it in a way that benefits the golf course

409
00:31:45,021 --> 00:31:47,020
because of the fertilizer aspect of it.

410
00:31:47,540 --> 00:31:50,940
But we're also doing our part to recycle products like this.

411
00:31:53,200 --> 00:31:58,260
For animal, vegetable, or mineral,

